    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d660">

                <pb facs="0735" n="723" xml:id="pg723"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890G.5151/7: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister Resident in Iraq</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Knabenshue</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Baghdad</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-12-18T15:00:00+03:00">December 18,
                            1940—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 19—1:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>121. The following is a confidential comment on my telegram No. 122, December 18,
                    4 p.m.<anchor n="15" xml:id="d660-f1"/> which will follow. While the British
                    decision may be for financial reasons it is generally believed that it is also
                    the beginning of a political and economic pressure to be increased in other
                    directions until Iraq’s attitude toward Britain becomes more cooperative.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Knabenshue</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d660-f1" n="15" xml:id="d660-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Infra</hi>.</p>
